the target language is: prolog.

% Demonstrates "the functions are:" (docs/user/reference/language.md §2.3).
%
% A function is an ordinary template of the form "... is *a value*", declared
% in a section of its own. Declaring it lets the sentence be written WITHOUT
% its last place wherever a value is expected: "the price of a cup with
% capacity 200" then means the price itself, so a condition about it reads as
% one phrase instead of two.
%
% Nothing else changes. The full sentence still works, whatever defines the
% function is an ordinary rule or fact, and a function may be a relation with
% more than one answer — "our currency" below is one value in the knowledge
% base and another in a scenario, and every sentence that uses it then has two
% answers.

the templates are:
    the capacity of *a cup* is *a number* ml.
    *a cup* is expensive.
    *a cup* is worth its price.
    the label of *a cup* is *a text*.

the functions are:
    the price of a cup with capacity *a number* ml is *an amount*.
    the price of a cup with capacity *a number* is *an amount*.
    our currency is *a text*.

the knowledge base functions includes:

% What defines a function is an ordinary rule: nothing about it is special.
the price of a cup with capacity a number ml is an amount
    if the amount is the number / 10.

the price of a cup with capacity a number is an amount
    if the price of a cup with capacity the number ml is the amount.

our currency is "EUR".

% The function written without its last place, in a comparison. The prefix here
% ends in a word ("ml"); the second function's ends in its own place, and both
% forms are read the same way.
a cup is expensive
    if the capacity of the cup is a capacity ml
    and the price of a cup with capacity the capacity ml > 10.

% The same application twice in one sentence is asked once — one goal, one
% value — so a function with several answers does not multiply them. Here the
% one price is compared twice: a thimble's 2 is not over 5.
a cup is worth its price
    if the capacity of the cup is a capacity ml
    and the price of a cup with capacity the capacity > 5
    and the price of a cup with capacity the capacity < 100.

% A function with no place of its own is a value with a name.
the label of a cup is a label
    if the capacity of the cup is a capacity ml
    and the label is our currency.

scenario one_currency is:
    the capacity of mug is 200 ml.
    the capacity of thimble is 20 ml.
    expensive expects answers ["mug is expensive"].
    worth expects answers ["mug is worth its price"].
    priced expects answers ["the price of a cup with capacity 200 ml is 20"].
    compact expects answers ["the price of a cup with capacity 200 is 20"].
    labels expects answers ["the label of mug is EUR", "the label of thimble is EUR"].

% A second value for a function of no places: every answer that reads it now
% has two readings, and the explanation shows which one it used.
scenario two_currencies is:
    the capacity of mug is 200 ml.
    our currency is "USD".
    labels expects answers ["the label of mug is EUR", "the label of mug is USD"].

query expensive is:
    which cup is expensive.

query worth is:
    which cup is worth its price.

query priced is:
    the price of a cup with capacity 200 ml is which amount.

query compact is:
    the price of a cup with capacity 200 is which amount.

query labels is:
    the label of which cup is which label.
